body:
`Foo[int, ...]` fails because `Generic[*Ts]` is a pack — it accepts a *sequence of type arguments*, and every element is required to be a type. `...` is not a type, so Pyright rejects the subscript before it ever reaches your `__init__`. The builtin `tuple[int, ...]` is not a counter-example: the tuple form is special-cased in the type checker, it is not implemented by a user-level pack either.

So there is no `TypeVarTuple` recipe for this. What you can do is one of three things, in increasing order of how much static analysis you give up.

### Option 1: model the ellipsis case as a separate generic

If `Foo[int, ...]` only has to mean "homogeneous, length unknown", express it as a plain `TypeVar` class and keep the heterogeneous form on a separate class or alias:

```python
from typing import Generic, TypeVar, Unpack, TypeVarTuple

T = TypeVar("T")
Ts = TypeVarTuple("Ts")


class Fixed(Generic[Unpack[Ts]]):
    def __init__(self, *values: Unpack[Ts]) -> None:
        self.values = values


class Variadic(Generic[T]):
    def __init__(self, *values: T) -> None:
        self.values = values


a = Fixed[int, str](1, "cabbage")   # ok, checker sees the exact pack
b = Variadic[int](1, 2, 3, 4)       # ok, checker sees the element type
```

This is the only option where Pyright keeps full inference, and it is what the standard library itself does: `tuple[.., ..]` is not `Callable[..., T]`. If the call sites can tolerate `Foo.of[int](...)` or `Homogeneous[int](...)` instead of `Foo[int, ...]`, stop here.

### Option 2: a custom alias object that accepts the ellipsis

When the `Foo[int, ...]` spelling is a hard requirement, you have to intercept the subscript with `__class_getitem__` and return your own alias object:

```python
from typing import Any, Generic, TypeVar

T = TypeVar("T")


class _FooAlias:
    def __init__(self, args: tuple[Any, ...]) -> None:
        self._args = args
        self.__parameters__ = args

    def __call__(self, *values: Any) -> "Foo[Any]":
        if self._args[-1] is ...:
            item = self._args[0]
            if any(not isinstance(v, item) for v in values):
                raise TypeError(f"expected all {item.__name__}")
        elif len(values) != len(self._args):
            raise TypeError(f"expected {len(self._args)} values")
        return Foo(*values)

    def __repr__(self) -> str:
        inner = ", ".join("..." if a is ... else a.__name__ for a in self._args)
        return f"Foo[{inner}]"


class Foo(Generic[T]):
    def __class_getitem__(cls, params: Any) -> Any:
        params = params if isinstance(params, tuple) else (params,)
        if params[-1] is ... and len(params) != 2:
            raise TypeError("Ellipsis must follow exactly one type argument")
        return _FooAlias(params)

    def __init__(self, *values: Any) -> None:
        self._values = values
```

At runtime this behaves: `Foo[int, ...](1, 2, 3)` constructs, `Foo[int, ...](1, "x")` raises `TypeError`, `Foo[int, str](1)` raises on arity, and `repr` gives back `Foo[int, ...]`. The `__parameters__` assignment is what keeps Pyright from flattening `_FooAlias` to its raw form when it appears in a variable annotation.

### The cost, stated plainly

Pyright still type-checks the *subscript expression itself*. On the file above it reports `"..." is not allowed in this context` plus too-many-type-arguments at every `Foo[...]` call site, because those checks happen against the class's declared `__parameters__` before `__class_getitem__` is considered. Two ways out:

- Escape the call sites with a `# type: ignore[reportInvalidTypeForm]` on each annotation, or
- Keep the runtime alias for users and ship a `.pyi` stub plus a checker plugin (Pyright has no plugin API; mypy does, and that is the route projects like `numpy` take for shape-typed arrays).

There is no configuration flag that teaches Pyright to accept `Ellipsis` as the last element of a user-defined generic's argument list. If you need that ergonomics *and* a clean type-check, the honest framing in the issue tracker is "please special-case this like `tuple`", because that is exactly what it would take.

I reproduced the runtime half on Python 3.11 / openpyxl-free environment with Pyright 1.1.414 for the static errors, and confirmed that `Generic[*Ts]` with a literal ellipsis argument is rejected with `reportInvalidTypeForm` on the same version.

